rsync for OpenVMS
=================

This is rsync for OpenVMS @ARCH@, built natively from openrsync (the
ISC-licensed rsync of OpenBSD and macOS), commit of @UPSTREAMDATE@, plus the
patches and VMS layer in the vms-rsync project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-rsync

Kit version: @KITVERSION@ (PCSI @PCSIVERSION@).  This is an early test
kit.  openrsync is free software under the ISC licence; see LICENSE.  The
manual is RSYNC.TXT; the notes below say where VMS differs.

What is installed
-----------------
    RSYNC$ROOT:[BIN]RSYNC.EXE             rsync
    RSYNC$ROOT:[000000]RSYNC$SETUP.COM    defines the rsync command
    RSYNC$ROOT:[DOC]                      this file, LICENSE, RSYNC.TXT
    SYS$STARTUP:RSYNC$STARTUP.COM         defines RSYNC$ROOT

zlib (-z) is linked in; nothing else is needed.  "-e ssh" uses VSI's
OpenSSH client (SSH$ROOT) when that is installed.

Setting up
----------
The kit installs RSYNC$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define RSYNC$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:RSYNC$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the command:

    $ @RSYNC$ROOT:[000000]RSYNC$SETUP.COM

What works
----------
rsync on VMS is a client: it copies to and from another system's rsync, and
from one VMS directory to another.

    $ rsync -av [.WWW] user@linuxhost:/srv/www/          push over ssh
    $ rsync -av user@linuxhost:/srv/www/ [.WWW]          pull over ssh
    $ rsync -av rsync://linuxhost/module/ [.MIRROR]      pull from a daemon
    $ rsync -av [.SRC] [.BACKUP]                         local copy

Tested against rsync 3.4.1 on Linux, with -a, -v, -n, -c, -z, --delete,
--partial, --progress, --link-dest and --keep-versions.  From an rsync://
daemon rsync can pull only: openrsync cannot push to a daemon or log in to
one.  VMS cannot yet be the remote end (rsync --server under VSI's sshd).

Arguments may be VMS specifications ([.DIR], DEV:[DIR], a logical name) or
paths (dir/sub/).  A directory given as a VMS specification means its
contents, as "dir/" does.

Text files
----------
A file of variable-length (or VFC) records with carriage control is sent as
its lines, each ending in LF, the way other systems store text.  Other files
(stream, fixed-length, undefined, and record files without carriage
control) are sent byte for byte.

A received file whose type is a text type is written as variable-length
records, so it reads as a normal VMS text file; anything else is written as
a Stream_LF file, exactly as sent.  The text types are those of VFTP:

    .TXT .C .H .CC .CPP .HPP .COM .MMS .MAR .FOR .F .F90 .PAS .BAS .COB .PL
    .PY .SH .HTML .HTM .CSS .JS .JSON .XML .YAML .YML .MD .RST .CSV .TSV .INI
    .CONF .CFG .LOG .LIS .MAP .OPT .SQL .DIFF .PATCH .MAK .AWK .SED .TEX
    .LIST .README .PEM .PUB

and a list of your own (separated by spaces or commas) replaces it:

    $ define RSYNC$TEXT_TYPES ".TXT,.COM,.LOG"

A text-type file that would not read back the same as records (one that
does not end with a newline, or has a line over 32,767 bytes) stays a
Stream_LF file.

Versions
--------
rsync never replaces a file in place: an updated file arrives as a new
version, and the older versions stay.  --keep-versions=N keeps only the
newest N versions of each file it updates:

    $ rsync -av "--keep-versions=3" user@linuxhost:/srv/www/ [.WWW]

--delete removes every version of a file the source no longer has; use -n
first to see what it would delete.

Names
-----
An ODS-5 disk keeps names as they are, including case.  Two names that
differ only in case (README.txt and Readme.TXT) are one file on VMS: the
first is written, the second is left out with a message, the rest of the
transfer goes on, and the exit status is 23.

Attributes
----------
-t sets the revision date; -p sets the protection from the mode bits (640
is S:RWD,O:RWD,G:R,W:).  Symbolic links are copied as links (-l).  Files
belong to the user running rsync: -o and -g, which -a includes, are
ignored.  -H is refused (VMS would make copies, not links).  Devices and
fifos are left out with a message.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to rsync in lower case,
so an unquoted -P arrives as -p.  Batch jobs use TRADITIONAL even when
interactive logins are set to EXTENDED.  Quote the options that use
capital letters ("-P"), or start the procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a failed run exits with an error-severity status, so ON ERROR and
IF .NOT. $STATUS work; rsync's exit code N (23 partial transfer, 12 protocol
error, 30 timeout, ...) is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.  Under a GNV shell, $?
is the exit code as on Unix.

Tracing
-------
    $ define RSYNC_VMS_TRACE 1

makes the VMS layer report each file operation on SYS$ERROR, for reports
of problems (https://github.com/issinoho/vms-rsync/issues).
